message.content:
{
  "summary": "This bill would create the Early Behavioral Intervention and Reporting Act, requiring every local education agency (LEA) and public charter school to use a computer-based system for tracking student \"early warning signs.\" Early warning signs include evidence of health, safety, or behavioral issues, such as substance abuse, mental health issues, suicidal ideation, self-harm, threats of violence, or instances of bullying and harassment. LEAs and public charter schools must train staff to identify these signs and enter them into the system. The system must maintain a database and immediately notify the school's threat assessment team when a sign is entered. Personnel receiving these notifications must review them and determine if further action is necessary. LEAs and public charter schools must submit annual reports to the department of education summarizing the collected data and any interventions provided, while complying with privacy laws including FERPA. The commissioner of education may withhold state funds from any LEA or public charter school that does not comply with these requirements. Additionally, the bill amends Tennessee Code Annotated Sections 49-6-2701 and 49-6-2702(b) to include public charter schools and their directors or governing bodies in provisions previously applying to LEAs and local boards of education. This act would take effect July 1, 2025."
}
